# 6. Tables and equations

Tables and equations are the two things a calculation report needs besides
text. About five minutes.
<!--{ te02 : markdown }-->
A beam of span <% L = 6, q = 12.5 %>**L = <% L %> m** carries a uniform load
**q = <% q %> kN/m**. This line names both values, `L` and `q`, for the
lines below.
<!--{ te03 : markdown }-->
## Tables

A Table line is edited as a grid of fields, as in Word:

| Keys and buttons | What they do |
|------------------|--------------|
| Tab, Shift+Tab | Go to the next or previous field; Tab in the last field adds a row |
| ↑, ↓ | Go to the field above or below |
| **+ Row**, **+ Column** | Insert a row below, or a column to the right |
| **− Row**, **− Column** | Delete the current row or column |
| Align buttons | Align the current column left, centre or right |

A field can hold formulas, between their brackets. To use a value elsewhere,
name it where you compute it: <% tutorial.tag "M: q * L ^ 2 / 8" %> gives the
value the name `M` *and* shows it.

**Your turn.** Select the empty line just above the check below and press
**Shift+T**: a new table. Write `Quantity` and `Value` in the header. In the
row below, write `Moment`, then press **Tab** and, in the second field, press
**Ctrl+E** and write `M: q * L ^ 2 / 8` between the brackets, then ` kNm`
after them. Align the second column to the right and press **Ctrl+Enter**.
<!--{ te04 : rich }-->

<!--{ te05 : formula }-->
<% tutorial.check (M == 56.25) %>
<!--{ te08 : markdown }-->
### Captions

A table's caption goes on its own line right after the table, between
`<caption>` and `</caption>`, and shows below the table:

| Data | Value |
|------|------:|
| Span L | <% L %> m |
| Load q | <% q %> kN/m |
<caption><b>Table 1:</b> Data of the beam.</caption>

Press **Enter** on this line to see how it is written, and **Escape** to
leave it. A caption needs a Markdown line: a Table line refuses it.

Try it on your table above: select it and press **m** to make it Markdown,
press **Enter**, add a line such as `<caption><b>Table 2:</b> Midspan
moment.</caption>` under the table and press **Ctrl+Enter**.
<!--{ te06 : markdown }-->
## Equations

Equations are written in **LaTeX**, the notation of scientific papers,
between dollar signs: `$...$` inside a sentence, `$$...$$` on lines of their
own. A formula inside an equation shows its value:

$$
M = \frac{q L^2}{8} = <% q * L ^ 2 / 8 %>\ \text{kNm}
$$

Press **Enter** on the equation above to see how it is written, and
**Escape** to leave it.

Try it: on an empty line at the end of this lesson, in rich text, type
`$\sigma = M / W$` or click **∑** in the toolbar. The equation is set as
you type; click it to change it.

More in the manual: [Table cells](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/editor/cell-types.html#table-cells)
and [Equations](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/editor/writing.html#equations).
